This information is typically in the Software Product Description
though it might also be in the Technical Guide.  From the V3.2C
SPD, the maximum file size for AdvFS is 128 GB.  This limit is the
result of knowing what works through testing.  I think the arch-
itectural limit is multiple terabytes.  With a file size that
large the maximum domain size has to be at least as large as
does the underlying domain.

In V4.0 this limit is raised, but I haven't seen the V4.0 SPD
to see what the limit is.

Multiple filesets allow segregation of file systems.  Each
file set has equal access to space of the domain, so a large
file set will use what it needs.  On the other hand a single
fileset clearly has access to all the space anyway.  But the
single fileset means putting all the users under a single
mount point.

A single fileset will have to be backed up at once, where multiple
filesets can be backed up separately.  This could affect how you
do backups.

alan.
****************************************************************************  
******************
I do not know the maximum size of an ADVFS, I have never seen it documented?

I can tell you this, I have a 2 RAID sets (5 RZ29b's) in a storageworks
cabinet. After accounting for the disk overhead to support RAID, I have some
16.3 GB's. I have two different ADVFS file systems using that space 16.3 GB.
I have had no problems.

I suggest you study about the metadata extent table used with ADVFS
domains. There are some issues with having a very large number of files
on a ADVFS domain, like 400,000+ causing problems if you do not keep the
ADVFS file system defragmented.

Thats the largest I have tried I believe it could be much bigger. The
problems with real large filesystems is how are you going to do your 
backups?

It really depends on what you are doing as to whether you want just a few
very large file systems or whether you want many small file systems.

You might also put smaller file systems together with LSM.

I have not really answered your questions directly. Hope my info help 
anyhow.

 Steve
****************************************************************************  
******************
****************************************************************************  
******************
