A week back I asked a few questions about DEC's RAID technoly, here's 
what I've got:
 - Performance: As one would expect, normal read performance and 
not-so-good write performance. Seems nobody has made any numerical tests.

 - Configurable: no go. Gyula Szokoly said "If you play it by the book, no",
so maybe there are some tricks ??? I've heard about RAID-5 subsystems 
which can calculate parity for a new disk 'on the fly'.

 - 3rd party disks: A few were using Seagate 9G drives, so it seems possible.

The reason I want to use AdvFS is configurability and performance. I 
want to keep all my files in one AdvFS domain (to keep it configurable), 
but this is quite a vulnerable setup, as the complete setup has to be 
reloaded if one disk crashes. 

As the RAID-5 setup seems to take away the good things about AdvFS, I 
think I'll pick LSM and mirroring instead.

