 Sheryl -

 I've had lots of fun in that area recently. We got a new Alpha 2000=20
4/233 last Oct. 3/95. Couldn't install the latest o/s at the time , v3.2c=20
as Datatel/Unidata wouldn't gaurantee that Unidata 3.3.2.15 would run on=20
it, so we had to get our supplier to install o/s v3.0b, if they could=20
find a copy. Then of course this wouldn't work with the new PCI=20
controller card so they had to install an EISA card.=20
 In early Nov./95 Unidata had a new version, 3.3.2.16, which would run=20
o/s v3.2c. So I did the undiata and o/s upgrade on the same day. For=20
being new to Digital Unix, Unidata and Datatel it was, umm well, an=20
experience. Actually the Unidata install went fairly easily following the=20
manual. The trouble came when I got to the 'updatevoc' section. Don't use=20
the '-A' option to traverse all user remote accounts, just run it in the=20
main remote accounts.
 The o/s upgrade went fairly smoothly also, except it somehow changed the=20
type of ethernet connection we had. As we have no terminals and everyone=20
logs on to the system via ethernet this could have been a big deal,=20
except we still hadn't gone 'live', and most users were at a local=20
Datatel Users Group Conference.
 We didn't go live until this April 1st. Bewteen Oct.2/95 and going live=20
we have had one o/s upgrade, a unidata upgrade, a hardware upgrade=20
(finally got to put our PCI card in - which took DEC 3 trips to get it in=20
right!), and 3 Colleague upgrades! For all those the unidata was the=20
easiest and the o/s was the next easiest to do.

 So, my advice is:
- for unidata - avoid 'updatevoc -A', just add new voc items to your user=20
remote accounts if you really need them.
- for Digital unix - if you do not have access to your network afterwards=20
check that your 'ewa0_mode' has not been changed. You check it from the=20
'>>>' prompt on the console when you first start the Alpha. Just type=20
'show ewa0_mode'. It will either say it is 'aui' or 'twisted-pair'.

Good luck!
